          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which microbial pathogenesis mechanism triggers a massive cytokine cascade that </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which microbial pathogenesis mechanism triggers a massive cytokine cascade that can lead to septic shock?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adhesin-mediated binding to host cell receptors</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Adhesins mediate bacterial attachment to specific host receptors, determining which organ gets infected (tissue tropism). They do not directly trigger the systemic inflammatory cascade that produces septic shock.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Type-three secretion system injection of effector proteins</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The type-three secretion system acts as a molecular syringe that injects proteins into individual host cells to facilitate invasion. It does not trigger the widespread cytokine storm that leads to septic shock.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Endotoxin (lipopolysaccharide) release by gram-negative bacteria</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Endotoxin (LPS), shed from the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria, activates innate immune cells and triggers a massive cytokine cascade. Left unchecked, this cascade produces septic shock — a direct mechanism-to-syndrome link.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Biofilm formation on tissue surfaces</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Biofilms make bacteria far more resistant to antibiotics and protect them from phagocytosis, but they do not directly trigger the acute systemic cytokine cascade responsible for septic shock.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the toxin section: exotoxins disrupt specific cell functions, while endotoxin (LPS) from gram-negative bacteria triggers a massive cytokine cascade that, if unchecked, produces septic shock."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Endotoxin (LPS) is the prototypical trigger of the cytokine cascade that drives septic shock — a key example of how each pathogenesis mechanism maps to a distinct clinical pattern."
                }
              ],
