          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A rice-field worker presents during monsoon with fever, bilateral reddening of t</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A rice-field worker presents during monsoon with fever, bilateral reddening of the conjunctivae, jaundice, and rapidly falling urine output. Which diagnosis does this clinical combination define?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Scrub typhus with multi-organ dysfunction</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Scrub typhus presents with a painless black eschar in concealed skin folds (groin, axillae, hairline) and endothelial vasculitis. Conjunctival suffusion — bilateral reddening without exudate — is characteristic of leptospirosis, not scrub typhus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Weil's disease (severe leptospirosis)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Weil's disease is the severe form of leptospirosis, defined by the triad of jaundice, acute kidney injury, and haemorrhage. Conjunctival suffusion is a hallmark vasculitis sign, and waterlogged-field exposure during monsoon fits the epidemiology perfectly.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Brucellosis with hepatorenal involvement</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Brucellosis characteristically causes undulating fever, drenching night sweats, and sacroiliac joint pain. Acute kidney injury with conjunctival suffusion is not a typical brucellosis presentation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Enteric fever complicated by hepatitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Enteric fever presents with stepwise fever and relative bradycardia, sometimes with rose spots. Conjunctival suffusion and acute kidney injury are not typical features and point strongly toward leptospirosis instead.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the leptospirosis section: conjunctival suffusion (bilateral reddening of the eyes without exudate) is a hallmark sign. Jaundice combined with acute kidney injury defines Weil's disease, the severe form requiring intravenous penicillin or ceftriaxone."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The combination of waterlogged-field exposure, conjunctival suffusion, jaundice, and acute kidney injury is the classic presentation of Weil's disease — the severe, life-threatening form of leptospirosis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
