          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 28-year-old man in an Indian hospital has blood-culture-confirmed enteric feve</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 28-year-old man in an Indian hospital has blood-culture-confirmed enteric fever. Considering India's current resistance landscape, which antibiotics are the appropriate first-line empirical choice?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fluoroquinolones were widely used for enteric fever, but multidrug-resistant Salmonella Typhi has made them unreliable as empirical first-line agents across much of India. Every prescription either drives resistance or counters it.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chloramphenicol and co-trimoxazole</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chloramphenicol and co-trimoxazole were first-generation anti-typhoid drugs. Multidrug-resistant Salmonella Typhi strains prevalent in India have rendered both unreliable for empirical use.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Azithromycin and ceftriaxone</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Azithromycin and ceftriaxone are now first-line for enteric fever in India, replacing older fluoroquinolone-based regimens. This reflects the high prevalence of multidrug-resistant Salmonella Typhi that limits ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin efficacy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Metronidazole and doxycycline</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Metronidazole covers amoebiasis and anaerobic infections; doxycycline covers rickettsial and zoonotic diseases. Neither has clinically useful activity against Salmonella Typhi.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the treatment framework: the narration states that multidrug-resistant Salmonella Typhi limits fluoroquinolones in India, and that azithromycin and ceftriaxone are now first-line for enteric fever."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Multidrug-resistant Salmonella Typhi in India has shifted first-line enteric fever treatment from fluoroquinolones to azithromycin and ceftriaxone — a key example of how local resistance patterns shape empirical prescribing."
                }
              ],
